WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.774 --> 00:00:15.677
"One thousand dollars," repeated Lawyer Tolman solemnly, "and here is the money."

2
00:00:15.678 --> 00:00:22.193
Young Gillian gave an amused laugh as he fingered the thin package of new fifty-dollar notes.

3
00:00:22.194 --> 00:00:28.858
"You heard the reading of your uncle's will," continued Lawyer Tolman, professionally impersonal in his tone.

4
00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:35.179
"I do not know if you paid much attention to its details. I must remind you of one.

5
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:43.306
You are required to render to us an account of the expenditure of this thousand dollars as soon as you have disposed of it.

6
00:00:43.307 --> 00:00:47.994
I trust that you will comply with the late Mr. Gillian's wishes."

7
00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:52.042
"You may depend upon it," said the young man politely.

8
00:00:52.043 --> 00:00:57.935
Gillian went to his club. There he hunted out one whom he called Old Bryson.

9
00:00:57.936 --> 00:01:02.902
Old Bryson was calm and forty and preferred to be left alone.

10
00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:12.904
He was in a corner reading a book, and when he saw Gillian approaching, he sighed, laid down his book, and took off his glasses.

11
00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:18.633
"Old Bryson, wake up," said Gillian. "I've a funny story to tell you."

12
00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:25.815
"I wish you would tell it to someone else," said Old Bryson. "You know how I hate your stories."

13
00:01:25.816 --> 00:01:32.785
"This is a better one than usual," said Gillian. "I've just come from my late uncle's legal firm.

14
00:01:32.786 --> 00:01:40.085
He leaves me a thousand dollars. Now, what can a man possibly do with a thousand dollars?"

15
00:01:40.086 --> 00:01:51.047
"I thought," said Old Bryson, showing as much interest as a bee shows in vinegar, "that the late Septimus Gillian was worth something like half a million."

16
00:01:51.048 --> 00:01:55.743
"He was," agreed Gillian, "and that's where the joke comes in.

17
00:01:55.744 --> 00:02:05.078
He's left part of it to the man who discovers a new type of bacteria and the rest to establish a hospital for doing away with it again.

18
00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:12.921
The butler and the housekeeper get a seal ring and ten dollars each. His nephew gets a thousand dollars."

19
00:02:12.922 --> 00:02:17.958
"You've always had plenty of money to spend," observed Old Bryson.

20
00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:24.663
"Tons," said Gillian. "Uncle was the fairy godmother as far as an allowance was concerned."

21
00:02:24.664 --> 00:02:27.595
"Any other heirs?" asked Old Bryson.

22
00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:37.547
"None." Gillian frowned uneasily. "There is a Miss Hayden, a ward of my uncle, who lived in his house. She's a quiet thing.

23
00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:42.952
I forgot to say that she was in on the seal-ring-and-ten-dollars joke too.

24
00:02:42.953 --> 00:02:49.522
Don't be superior and insulting, Old Bryson—tell me what a fellow can do with a thousand dollars."

25
00:02:49.523 --> 00:02:58.874
Old Bryson rubbed his glasses and smiled. And when Old Bryson smiled, Gillian knew that he intended to be more offensive than ever.

26
00:02:58.875 --> 00:03:07.731
"A thousand dollars," he said, "means much or little. One man may buy a happy home with it and laugh at Rockefeller.

27
00:03:07.732 --> 00:03:13.055
Another could send his wife for a vacation with it and save her life.

28
00:03:13.056 --> 00:03:22.383
A thousand dollars would buy pure milk for one hundred babies during June, July, and August and save fifty of their lives.

29
00:03:22.384 --> 00:03:26.427
It would provide an education to an ambitious boy."

30
00:03:26.428 --> 00:03:33.395
"People might like you, Old Bryson," said Gillian, always unruffled, "if you wouldn't moralize.

31
00:03:33.396 --> 00:03:37.517
I asked you to tell me what I could do with a thousand dollars."

32
00:03:37.518 --> 00:03:40.982
"You?" said Bryson with a gentle laugh.

33
00:03:40.983 --> 00:03:50.269
"Why, Bobby Gillian, there's only one logical thing you could do. You can go buy Miss Lotta Lauriere a diamond pendant."

34
00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:57.864
"Thanks," said Gillian, rising. "I thought I could depend upon you, Old Bryson. You've got the right idea.

35
00:03:57.865 --> 00:04:04.935
I wanted to spend the money in a lump sum, for I've got to turn in an account for it, and I hate itemizing."

36
00:04:04.936 --> 00:04:09.806
Gillian phoned for a cab and said to the driver: "The Columbine Theatre."

37
00:04:09.807 --> 00:04:17.878
Miss Lotta Lauriere was almost ready for her call at a crowded matinee, when her dresser mentioned the name of Mr. Gillian.

38
00:04:17.879 --> 00:04:23.503
"Now, what is it, Bobby?" said Miss Lauriere. "I'm going on in two minutes."

39
00:04:23.504 --> 00:04:29.619
"It won't take two minutes. What do you say to a little pendant? I can spend a grand."

40
00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:38.119
"Oh, just as you say," replied Miss Lauriere. "Say, Bobby, did you see that necklace Della Stacey had on the other night?

41
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.956
Twenty-two hundred dollars it cost at Tiffany's. But—"

42
00:04:41.957 --> 00:04:45.956
"Miss Lauriere for the opening chorus!" cried the callboy.

43
00:04:45.957 --> 00:04:49.206
Gillian strolled out to where his cab was waiting.

44
00:04:49.207 --> 00:04:53.413
"What would you do with a thousand dollars?" he asked the driver.

45
00:04:53.414 --> 00:05:02.735
"Open a saloon," said the cabby promptly. "I know a place I could take money in with both hands. It's a four-story brick on a corner.

46
00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:11.447
I've got it figured out: second story, Chinese food; third floor, manicures; fourth floor, poolroom.

47
00:05:11.448 --> 00:05:14.020
If you're thinking of putting up the capital—"

48
00:05:14.021 --> 00:05:22.577
"Oh no," said Gillian, "I merely asked from curiosity. I'll pay you by the hour. Drive 'til I tell you to stop."

49
00:05:22.578 --> 00:05:25.950
Eight blocks down Broadway, Gillian got out.

50
00:05:25.951 --> 00:05:32.856
A blind man sat upon a stool on the sidewalk selling pencils. Gillian went out and stood before him.

51
00:05:32.857 --> 00:05:39.596
"Excuse me," he said, "but would you mind telling me what you would do if you had a thousand dollars?"

52
00:05:39.597 --> 00:05:43.358
"Take a look at that, if you like," said the blind man.

53
00:05:43.359 --> 00:05:51.921
He drew a bank deposit book from his coat pocket and held it out. It showed a total of $1,785.

54
00:05:51.922 --> 00:06:01.147
Gillian got back into the cab. "I forgot something," he said. "You may drive to the law offices of Tolman & Sharp, at Broadway."

55
00:06:01.148 --> 00:06:07.662
Back at the office, Tolman looked at him hostilely and inquiringly through his gold-rimmed glasses.

56
00:06:07.663 --> 00:06:13.079
"I beg your pardon," said Gillian cheerfully, "but may I ask you a question?

57
00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:21.118
It is not an impertinent one, I hope. Was Miss Hayden left anything by my uncle's will besides the ring and the ten dollars?"

58
00:06:21.119 --> 00:06:23.682
"Nothing," said Mr. Tolman.

59
00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:32.429
"I thank you very much, sir," said Gillian, and on he went to his cab. He gave the driver the address of his late uncle's home.

60
00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:39.266
Miss Hayden was writing letters in the library. She was small and slender and clothed in black.

61
00:06:39.267 --> 00:06:46.329
But you would have noticed her eyes. Gillian drifted in with his air of regarding the world as inconsequential.

62
00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:52.311
"I've just come from old Tolman's," he explained. "They've been going over the papers down there.

63
00:06:52.312 --> 00:06:59.644
They found a"—Gillian searched his memory for a legal term—"they found a postscript or something to the will.

64
00:06:59.645 --> 00:07:05.667
It seems that the old boy willed you a thousand dollars. Tolman asked me to bring you the money.

65
00:07:05.668 --> 00:07:11.727
Here it is. You'd better count it to see if it's right." Gillian laid the money on the desk.

66
00:07:11.728 --> 00:07:17.329
Miss Hayden turned white. "Oh!" she said, and again, "Oh!"

67
00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:20.771
Gillian half turned and looked out the window.

68
00:07:20.772 --> 00:07:26.522
"I suppose, of course," he said, in a low voice, "that you know I love you."

69
00:07:26.523 --> 00:07:30.559
"I'm sorry," said Miss Hayden, taking up her money.

70
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:36.000
"So there is no hope for us?" asked Gillian, almost lightheartedly.

71
00:07:36.001 --> 00:07:38.889
"I am sorry," she said again.

72
00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:47.161
"May I write a note?" asked Gillian, with a smile. She supplied him with paper and pen, and then went back to her desk.

73
00:07:47.162 --> 00:07:52.818
Gillian made out his account of his expenditure of the thousand dollars in these words:

74
00:07:52.819 --> 00:08:03.656
"Paid by the black sheep, Robert Gillian, one thousand dollars on account of the eternal happiness owed by heaven to the best and dearest woman on earth."

75
00:08:03.657 --> 00:08:08.359
Gillian slipped his writing into an envelope, bowed, and went his way.

76
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.785
His cab stopped again at the offices of Tolman & Sharp.

77
00:08:12.786 --> 00:08:20.700
"I have spent the thousand dollars," he said cheerily to Tolman. He tossed a white envelope on the lawyer's table.

78
00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:25.112
"You will find there an account, sir, of the vanishing of the dollars."

79
00:08:25.113 --> 00:08:30.713
Without touching the envelope, Mr. Tolman went to a door and called his partner, Sharp.

80
00:08:30.714 --> 00:08:34.308
Together they explored the caverns of an immense safe.

81
00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:43.604
From the safe, they dragged a big envelope sealed with wax, which they invaded, and wagged their venerable heads together over its contents.

82
00:08:43.605 --> 00:08:46.151
Then Tolman became spokesman.

83
00:08:46.152 --> 00:08:51.365
"Mr. Gillian," he said formally, "there was a supplement to your uncle's will.

84
00:08:51.366 --> 00:09:01.144
It was entrusted to us privately, with instructions that it not be opened until you had furnished us with a full account of your handling of the thousand dollars.

85
00:09:01.145 --> 00:09:04.991
I will acquaint you with the spirit of its contents.

86
00:09:04.992 --> 00:09:15.668
"In the event that your expenditure of the thousand dollars demonstrates that you possess any of the qualifications that deserve reward, much benefit will come to you.

87
00:09:15.669 --> 00:09:18.570
Mr. Sharp and I are named as the judges.

88
00:09:18.571 --> 00:09:28.803
If your disposal of the money in question has been prudent, wise, or unselfish, it is in our power to hand you fifty thousand dollars.

89
00:09:28.804 --> 00:09:36.660
But if you have used this money foolishly, the fifty thousand is to be paid to Miriam Hayden without delay.

90
00:09:36.661 --> 00:09:43.398
Now, Mr. Gillian, Mr. Sharp and I will examine your account in regard to the thousand dollars."

91
00:09:43.399 --> 00:09:49.009
Mr. Tolman reached for the envelope. Gillian was a little quicker in taking it up.

92
00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:54.335
He slowly tore the account and its cover into strips and dropped them into his pocket.

93
00:09:54.336 --> 00:10:01.210
"It's all right," he said, smilingly. "There isn't a bit of need to bother you with this.

94
00:10:01.211 --> 00:10:06.026
I lost the thousand dollars on the horse races. Good day, gentlemen."

95
00:10:06.027 --> 00:10:15.226
Tolman and Sharp shook their heads mournfully at each other when Gillian left, for they heard him whistling gaily in the hallway as he waited for the elevator.
